Hi @RLWA32,

Thanks for asking. I checked the results from my additional tests, and I was able to reproduce the behavior you described for all three VirtualAlloc protection settings in a same-process scenario.

I allocated three regions using VirtualAlloc, initially with PAGE_READWRITE, and then changed two of them to PAGE_READONLY and PAGE_EXECUTE_READ using VirtualProtect.

Here are the results:

```text
Handle: PROCESS_VM_WRITE only
PAGE_READWRITE                 ok=0  written=0  error=5  value=0x11
PAGE_READONLY                  ok=0  written=0  error=5  value=0x22
PAGE_EXECUTE_READ              ok=0  written=0  error=5  value=0x33

Handle: PROCESS_VM_WRITE | PROCESS_VM_OPERATION
PAGE_READWRITE                 ok=1  written=1  error=0  value=0xA1
PAGE_READONLY                  ok=0  written=0  error=998  value=0x22
PAGE_EXECUTE_READ              ok=1  written=1  error=0  value=0xC1
```

This matches your observations, including the ERROR_NOACCESS result when writing to PAGE_READONLY memory and the successful write to PAGE_EXECUTE_READ memory when PROCESS_VM_OPERATION is included.

My original cross-process reproduction was limited to a PAGE_READWRITE region allocated with VirtualAlloc, where WriteProcessMemory succeeded without PROCESS_VM_OPERATION. I haven't yet extended the tests to cover HeapAlloc, GlobalAlloc, LocalAlloc, or variables in separate memory sections. I also haven't repeated the three protection tests across separate processes.

So far, the results confirm your observations for the three VirtualAlloc protection settings in the same-process scenario, while the behavior you initially reported for the cross-process case remains something I haven't fully investigated.
